# BHOOMI-NETRA Citizen — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in the values from your Supabase project settings
# (Project Settings → API). `.env` is gitignored; this template is not.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix, and what it costs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Expo only inlines variables with this prefix into the client bundle, and it
# inlines them as plain text. The Expo docs are blunt about the consequence:
# these values "will be visible in plain-text in your compiled application".
# Anyone who downloads the APK can read both lines below.
#
# That is acceptable for exactly these two values and nothing else. A Supabase
# anon key is designed to be public; row level security is what protects the
# data, and 006_citizen_tables.sql grants the anon role SELECT on shelters, risk
# zones and routes and nothing more.
#
# Never put a service role key, a database password or an SMS gateway credential
# in this file. There is no EXPO_PUBLIC_ variable that is private, so anything
# that must stay secret belongs behind an Edge Function instead.
#
# One more Expo constraint worth knowing before you rename anything: these are
# read in src/services/supabase.ts as static `process.env.NAME` property
# accesses, because that is the only form the transform recognises. Bracket
# access and destructuring silently produce `undefined` in a release build.

EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The street map under the zone overlay
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# All three of these are OPTIONAL. Leave them out entirely and the app uses a
# built-in chain of keyless sources, tries each in turn at runtime, and credits
# whichever one actually answered. There is nothing to sign up for.
#
# Set EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_URL and your source goes in FRONT of that chain rather
# than replacing it, so a mistyped key or an expired plan degrades to a working
# map instead of a blank one. Set it to an empty string to ship with no basemap
# at all — the map draws its own graticule and says so in the credit line, which
# is a legitimate choice for a low-data build.
#
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_URL          XYZ template(s). `{z}/{x}/{y}` required; `{s}`
#                                 (subdomain a-d) and `{r}` (retina) optional.
#                                 Separate multiple layers with `|` — artwork
#                                 first, labels second, drawn in that order.
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_ATTRIBUTION  Rendered in the credit line. Not optional in
#                                 law; changing the source without changing this
#                                 is how a licence gets breached quietly.
#   EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_SCHEME       `dark` (default) or `light`. Not a taste
#                                 setting: every mark on this map is cream on a
#                                 night ground, so a light basemap is pushed much
#                                 further back before a cream zone label can be
#                                 read over it.
#
# On API keys, because this is the part that bites:
#
#   A key in EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_URL ships in plain text inside the APK, exactly
#   like the anon key above — but unlike the anon key it is NOT designed to be
#   public. Anyone can extract it and spend your quota. Restrict it by referrer
#   or by bundle id in your provider's dashboard, or proxy tiles through a
#   Supabase Edge Function and keep the key server-side. For a demo build the
#   exposure is survivable; for a real deployment it is not.
#
# Sources worth knowing about:
#
#   Esri Dark Gray Canvas — the built-in default, and no key. Drawn to sit under
#     data: muted greys, no saturated colour to fight a terracotta hazard wash.
#     Two halves, artwork then labels, and note Esri orders the path {z}/{y}/{x}:
#     .../Canvas/World_Dark_Gray_Base/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}|.../Canvas/World_Dark_Gray_Reference/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}
#
#   Bhuvan (ISRO / NRSC) — the right answer for an Indian government submission:
#     Indian-hosted, Survey of India lineage, no third-party dependency. Check
#     its WMTS endpoint against your own account before you trust it.
#
#   CARTO dark_all — the nicest artwork of the lot, and what this file used to
#     default to. It now REQUIRES a key and stamps "API KEY REQUIRED" diagonally
#     across every unauthenticated tile. Free tier is generous for
#     non-commercial use; CARTO is also migrating away from raster toward vector.
#     https://{s}.basemaps.cartocdn.com/dark_all/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY
#
#   Stadia, MapTiler, Thunderforest — all have good dark rasters, all need a free
#     key. Same exposure warning as CARTO.
#
#   OpenStreetMap standard — the last resort in the built-in chain, and the one
#     URL certain to answer. Light artwork under a heavy scrim: legible, not
#     beautiful. OSM's tile usage policy does not permit app traffic at scale, so
#     it is fine for a demo and not fine shipped. Self-host or use MapTiler.

# EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_URL=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_TILE_SCHEME=dark
